<project>
    <title>Cryptanalysis of substitution cipher</title>
<p>
    Eve has intercepted the following ciphertext message, and suspects it was encrypted using a substitution cipher. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
        c=''
    </input>
    <output>
    
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    Here is function that performs frequency analysis on a string.
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
def freq_anal(s):
    s=list(s)
    table={x:s.count(x) for x in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'}
    bigrams={}
    trigrams={}
    for i in range(len(s)-1):
        di=s[i]+s[i+1]
        bigrams[di]=bigrams.get(di,0)+1
    for i in range(len(s)-2):
        ti=s[i]+s[i+1]+s[i+2]
        trigrams[ti]=trigrams.get(ti,0)+1
    return table, bigrams, trigrams
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    Let's look at the top ten most common letters, bigrams, and trigrams.
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
l,b,t=freq_anal(c)
for x in sorted(l.keys(),key=lambda k:l[k], reverse=True)[:10]:
    print(x, l[x]/len(c))
for x in sorted(b.keys(),key=lambda k:b[k], reverse=True)[:15]:
    print(x, b[x])
for x in sorted(t.keys(),key=lambda k:t[k], reverse=True)[:15]:
    print(x, t[x])
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    The the next function allows us to make guesses about what the letter substitutions are and update our guess at the deciphered text. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
 t=['_']*len(c)
guesses={}
def guess(x,y):
    for i in range(len(c)):
        if c[i]==x: 
            t[i]=y
            guesses[x]=y
    print('Guesses: ', guesses)
    k=50
    for i in range(len(c)//50):
        print('\n'+c[i*k:(i+1)*k]+'\n')
        tstr=str()
        for x in t[i*k: (i+1)*k]: tstr=tstr+x
        print(tstr)
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
