</project>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_vignere_cipher">
    <title>Vigenère cipher</title>
    <p>
        What made the substitution cipher vulnerable to frequency analysis is that the encryption was defined on the level of single letters. We call this a <em>monographic</em> cryptosystem. A <em>polygraphic</em> (<em>block</em>) cryptosystem, in contrast, defines an encryption method on a block, or list of letters. The <em>Vignère cipher</em> defined below is a classic example of a block cipher that at first glance appears to be invulnerable to frequency analysis, and indeed was long considered to be unbreakable. The Vignère cipher was popularized by the French diplomat Blaise de Vignère in the 16th century, when during a trip to Rome he became familiar with some of the cryptographic techniques of Leon Alberti, a 15th century Italian scholar and quintessential Renaissance man (poet, composer, painter, <etc/>).
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="d_vignere">
    <title>Vigenère cipher</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>\mathcal{A}=\{0,1,\dots, 25\}</m>, and let <m>n</m> be a positive integer. Define 
    <md>
        \mathcal{K}=\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{A}^n
    </md>, 
    and encryption methods <m>E</m> and <m>D</m> as <md>
        <mrow>E_{(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)}(p_1,p_2,\dots, p_n) \amp = (p_1+k_1\boldmod{26}, p_2+k_2\boldmod{26},\dots p_n+k_n\boldmod{26})</mrow>
        <mrow>D_{(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)}(c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n) \amp = (c_1-k_1\boldmod{26}, c_2-k_2\boldmod{26},\dots c_n-k_n\boldmod{26})</mrow>
    </md>
    for each key <m>k=(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)\in \mathcal{K}</m>, plaintext <m>p=(p_1,p_2,\dots, p_n)\in \mathcal{P}</m> and ciphertext <m>c=(c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n)\in \mathcal{C}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        In this setting a key <m>k=(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)</m> is called a <term>keyword</term>. 
    </p>
    </statement>
    </definition>
<project xml:id="sage_vignere">
        <title>Vigenère cipher: encryption and decryption</title>
        <p>
           Again, using Sage we can easily implement the Vigenère cipher. 
        </p>
        <sage>
            <input>
                AlphaNum={}
for x in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':
    AlphaNum[x]=ord(x)-97
NumAlpha={y:x for (x,y) in AlphaNum.items()}

def vig_enc(m,k):
    r=len(k)
    seq=[]
    for x in k:
        seq.append(AlphaNum[x])
    c=''
    for i in range(len(m)):
        c=c+NumAlpha[(AlphaNum[m[i]]+seq[i%r])%26]
    return c
def vig_dec(c,k):
    kbar=''
    for x in k:
        kbar=kbar+NumAlpha[-AlphaNum[x]%26]
    return vig_enc(c,kbar)
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage>
        <p>
            Try encrypting and decrpyting a message of your choosing using the Vignère cipher and a keyword of your choosing. 
        </p>
        <sage>
            <input>
                
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage>
        <p>
            The following is an intercepted ciphertext using the Vignère cipher and the keyword <c>poetry</c>. Find the secret message!
        </p>
        <sage>
            <input>
                c=''
            </input>
            <output>
                
