        </sage>  
        <sage>
            <input>
                
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage> 
    </project>      
<p>
    The Vignère cipher was long believed to be uncrackable. Indeed it was dubbed the <foreign>le chiffre indéchiffrable</foreign>. However, once the the <em>length</em> of the keyword is determined, it is not difficult to determine the keyword itself using frequency analysis on substrings of the ciphertext. Furthermore, using the <em>Kasiski method</em>, we can often determine the length of the keyword by computing distances between repeated substrings (<eg/>, between repeated trigrams) in the ciphertext and looking for common factors of these distances. It likely that the keyword length is one of these common factors. Simon Singh has created a nice interactive demonstration of this method on his cryptography website <url href="https://www.simonsingh.net/The_Black_Chamber/vigenere_cracking.html">The Black Chamber</url>. Try using his interactive to decipher the following Vignère-encrypted ciphertext.
<listing><program></program></listing>
In 1922 William Friedman developed an alternative and more effective statistical tool for cracking Vigenere and other periodic substitution ciphers: the index of coincidence. 
</p>
<definition xml:id="d_index_coincidence">
<title>Index of coincidence</title>
<statement>
<p>
  Let <m>s</m> be a string of length <m>n</m> consisting of letters from our alphabet.
  For all <m>0\leq i\leq 25</m>, let <m>F_{i}</m> be the number of times the <m>i</m>-th letter of the alphabet appears in <m>S</m>.
We define the <term>index of coincidence</term> of <m>s</m> to be
  <md>
    \operatorname{IndCo}(s)=\sum_{i=0}^{25}\frac{F_{i}}{n}\frac{F_{i}-1}{n-1}=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum_{i=0}^{25}F_{i}(F_{i}-1).
  </md>
  Similarly given two strings <m>s</m>, <m>t</m> of length <m>n</m> and <m>m</m>, respectively, and with respective counts <m>F_{i}</m> and <m>F_{i}'</m> for the <m>i</m>-th letter of the alphabet, we define the <term>mutual index of coincidence</term> of <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> to be
  <md>
    \operatorname{MutIndCo}(s,t)=\sum_{i=0}^{25}\frac{F_{i}}{n}\cdot\frac{F_{i}'}{m}=\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=0}^{25}F_{i}F_{i}'.
  </md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>
    An elementary probabilistic argument shows that <m>\operatorname{IndCo}(s)</m> is the probability that two randomly selected letters from <m>s</m> are the same, and that <m>\operatorname{MutIndCo}(s,t)</m> is the probability that randomly selecting an element from <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> produces the same letter.
</p>
<p>
  Suppose <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> are strings taken from <em>natural English language</em>.
  As <m>n,m\rightarrow\infty</m> the relative frequencies <m>F_{i}/n</m>, <m>F'_{i}/m</m> and <m>(F_{i}-1)/(n-1)</m> all approach the natural relative frequency of the <m>i</m>-th letter of the alphabet, call it <m>p_{i}</m>.
  (Thus <m>p_{0}=.082, p_{1}=.015,\dots, p_{25}=0.001)</m>.) We see then as <m>n,m\rightarrow\infty</m> both <m>\text{IndCo}(s)</m> and <m>\text{MutIndCo}(s,t)</m> approach <m>p_{0}^{2}+p_{1}^{2}+\cdots +p_{25}^{2}\approx 0.0656</m>.
</p>

<p>
  There is a clear connection here with linear algebra and inner products.
  Define the vector
  <md>
    \mathbf{p}=(p_{0},p_{1},\dots, p_{25})=(0.082,0.015,.028,\dots, 0.001,0.020,0.001)
  </md>
  Then using the dot product, we see that <m>p_{0}^{2}+p_{1}^{2}+\cdots +p_{25}^{2}=\mathbf{p}\cdot\mathbf{p}</m>.
</p>

<p>
  Now suppose <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> are <em>random</em> strings of characters.
  Then in this case as <m>n,m\rightarrow\infty</m> the various relative frequencies all approach <m>1/26</m>, and thus both <m>\text{IndCo}(s)</m> and <m>\text{MutIndCo}(s,t)</m> approach <m>\frac{1}{26^{2}}+\frac{1}{26^{2}}+\cdots +\frac{1}{26^{2}}=\frac{1}{26}\approx 0.0385</m>.
</p>

<p>
  As in Case 1, we can define the vector <m>\mathbf{r}=(\frac{1}{26},\dots, \frac{1}{26})</m>.
  Then we see that for random sequences of characters the various indices of coincidence approach <m>\mathbf{r}\cdot\mathbf{r}</m>.
</p>

<p>
  Continuing with this line of thought, suppose <m>s</m> is the result of taking some natural English and performing a simple substitution by <m>\sigma</m>; similarly suppose <m>t</m> is the result of taking some natural English and performing a simple substitution by <m>\tau</m>.
  Then as <m>n,m\rightarrow\infty</m> the various relative frequencies will still approach the natural relative frequencies, but with a reordering.
</p>

<p>
  Now taking the vector <m>\mathbf{p}</m> from above, define <m>\mathbf{p}^{\sigma}</m> to be the same vector, but with entries permuted according to <m>\sigma</m>.
  Define <m>\mathbf{p}^{\tau}</m> similarly.
  Then as <m>n,m\rightarrow\infty</m>, we have
  <md>
    <mrow> \text{IndCo}(s),\text{IndCo}(t)\amp\rightarrow \mathbf{p}^{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{p}^{\sigma}\approx 0.0656 </mrow>
    <mrow> \text{MutIndCo}(s,t) \amp \rightarrow \mathbf{p}^{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{p}^{\tau} </mrow>
  </md>.
  The last limit computation gives us a simple test to see whether <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> have used the same substitution permuation: when <m>\sigma=\tau</m>, we should have <m>\text{MulIndCo}(s,t)\approx 0.0656</m>; otherwise we should have <m>\text{MulIndCo}(s,t)&lt; 0.0656</m>.
  The latter oberservation comes from Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, which says that
  <md>
    \mathbf{p}^{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{p}^{\tau}\leq \vert\vert \mathbf{p}^{\sigma}\vert\vert \vert\vert\mathbf{p}^{\tau}\vert\vert.
  </md>
  If we restrict our attention to shift ciphers, then in this modern age we can just compute a <m>(26)\times(26)</m> table of the various values <m>\mathbf{p}^{s_i}\cdot\mathbf{p}^{s_j}</m> (where <m>s_{l}</m> denotes the shift by <m>l</m> substitution) and compare our empirical result with these values.
</p>
<project>
    <title>Friedman cryptanalysis of the Vigenère cipher</title>
    <p>
    The Sage cell below contains functions for implementing the Vignère cipher, performing Caesar shift ciphers, and partitioning a string into substrings. 
    </p>
    <sage>
        <input>
AlphaNum={}
for x in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':
    AlphaNum[x]=ord(x)-97
NumAlpha={y:x for (x,y) in AlphaNum.items()}

def vig_enc(m,k):
    r=len(k)
    seq=[]
    for x in k:
        seq.append(AlphaNum[x])
    c=''
    for i in range(len(m)):
        c=c+NumAlpha[(AlphaNum[m[i]]+seq[i%r])%26]
    return c
def vig_dec(c,k):
    kbar=''
    for x in k:
        kbar=kbar+NumAlpha[-AlphaNum[x]%26]
    return vig_enc(c,kbar)
def shift(s,i):
    t=''
    for x in s:
        t=t+NumAlpha[(AlphaNum[x]+i)%26]
    return t
def substr(s,k):
    """Partition string s into k substrings"""
    n=len(s)
    substrings=[]
    for i in range(k):
        substring=''
        for j in range(i,n,k):
            substring=substring+s[j]
        substrings.append(substring)
    return substrings
